<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=bessemer-process">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bessemer process</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bessemer process makes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> from molten pig iron by blowing air through it to burn off excess carbon and other impurities; it was patented by the Englishman Henry Bessemer in 1856. It was the first inexpensive industrial process for mass-producing steel, converting three to five tons of iron in 10 to 20 minutes rather than at least a full day. By cutting the cost of steel from £40 to £6–7 per long ton, it made steel about as cheap as wrought iron, and railways in particular turned to steel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bessemer-process.jpg" alt="Bessemer process" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bessemer_converter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Henry Bessemer later wrote that the idea came from a conversation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> in 1854 about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> needed for better artillery, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>. At that time steel was used only for small items such as cutlery and tools and was too expensive for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>. He began work in January 1855, filed his first related patent in October of that year and patented the method in 1856. The American inventor William Kelly had experimented with a similar method in the early to mid-1850s and was awarded a priority patent in 1857; whether both invented the same process remains controversial, and Kelly's version is thought to have been less developed.</p><p>Bessemer licensed the process to four ironmasters for £27,000, but they could not make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> of the promised quality, and he bought the licences back for £32,500. The fault lay in impurities in the iron. The English metallurgist Robert Forester Mushet found the remedy: burn off nearly all impurities and carbon, then add carbon and manganese in the form of spiegeleisen. Bessemer acquired Mushet's patent after it lapsed. Commercial production began in Sheffield in 1858, using imported Swedish charcoal pig iron.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Air is blown through molten pig iron in a converter, which oxidises silicon, manganese and carbon. The oxides escape as gas or form slag, and the heat of oxidation keeps the iron molten. The blow lasted about 20 minutes, and its progress was judged by the flame at the mouth of the converter. Carbon was then added back. A converter treated a batch of 5 to 30 tons, and converters usually worked in pairs. Bessemer himself used a ganister sandstone lining, the acid process. In 1878 Sidney Gilchrist Thomas, with his cousin Percy Gilchrist, patented a dolomite or limestone lining that removed phosphorus, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilchrist-thomas-process">basic Bessemer process</a>.</p><p>In the United States Alexander Lyman Holley secured a licence in 1863 for John F. Winslow and John Augustus Griswold, and a mill opened at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Andrew Carnegie's Edgar Thomson Steel Works opened in 1875, and most American Bessemer steel went into railroad rails.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">Steel</a> rails lasted ten times longer than iron rails and carried heavier locomotives. Quality problems, including brittleness caused by nitrogen in the air blast, kept Bessemer steel out of many structural uses, for which open-hearth steel was suitable. By 1895 observers in the UK judged the process to be in decline. The basic process lasted longer, especially in continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where ores were rich in phosphorus. Commercial Bessemer production in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> stopped in 1968, and basic oxygen steelmaking, in essence an improved Bessemer process, replaced it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bessemer process” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1856</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Henry Bessemer</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>United States (William Kelly) (1851)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The American William Kelly is said to have found the process in 1851; whether both men invented the same process is disputed.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cast-iron">Cast iron<span>8th century BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/skyscraper">Skyscraper<span>1885</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=bessemer-process">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
